    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d48">

                <pb facs="0182" n="97" xml:id="pg97"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123D322/77: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Poland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gibson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-29T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 29, 1920,
                                5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>145. Instruct Dawson Dantzig to apply to High Commissioner for Free City of
                    Dantzig for recognition for himself and Halstead as Consul and Vice-Consul
                    respectively. See Dawson’s letter January 2, 1920, to you. As High Commissioner
                    cannot of right refuse recognition to American Consular Officers, it is
                    understood that such application is mere formality.</p>
                <p>Report promptly to Department conclusion of treaty between Poland and Dantzig
                    provided for by Article 104 of German treaty, as according to Despatch No. 80
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">8</hi>] of December 29, 1919, from American
                    Commissioner, Dantzig,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d48-f1"/> such treaty will give
                    Poland power to grant exequaturs to foreign consuls at Dantzig.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Colby</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
